| Time clause            | Main clause   | Emphasis                                                                    | Explanation                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      |
| :--------------------- | :------------ | :-------------------------------------------------------------------------- | :--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **When you come**      | I'll call him | **Occurrence** of the "coming" event.                                       | As soon as you arrive (or are in the process of arriving), I will call him. Focus is on the immediate proximity of the two actions.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |
| **When you have finished** | I'll call him | **Completion** of the "finishing" action.                                   | Only *after* you have entirely completed whatever task you're doing, will I call him. The "finishing" must be 100% done first.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |
| **When I shower**      | I'll call him | **Ambiguous** (between occurrence and completion, depending on context). | <br>**Option A (Present Simple, common):** Implies "While I'm showering" or "As soon as I begin to shower." (Less likely for "call him" unless it means 'I'll call him *after* I shower' - which is where the ambiguity arises if not clarified by context/timing). <br><br>**Option B (To clarify completion, use Present Perfect):** "When I **have showered**, I'll call him." This explicitly means: *after* I have finished my shower, then I will make the call. |

**Key takeaway for "When I shower":**

*   "When I shower" *could* mean "while I am in the shower" (simultaneous) or "after I have finished showering" (subsequent).
*   If you intend the action to happen *after* the shower is complete, using **"When I have showered..."** makes that completion explicit and unambiguous.
*   If you intend to imply "while I am showering," then "While I am showering..." is a clearer choice.

















